// The Arch logo in Arch's own blue, and nothing else changed.
//
// fastfetch draws the logo out of the sixteen-colour palette by default, so the
// arch mark came out in whichever spaceduck colour occupies that slot. The
// obvious fix is the wrong one: moving a colour in the palette to suit one logo
// would change every program that asks for that slot, including the text
// console, whose table has to stay in step with kitty's for the tuigreet preview
// to mean anything.
//
// `38;2;R;G;B` is a 24-bit SGR sequence, which kitty renders exactly as given
// without consulting the palette at all, so the mark is the only thing on screen
// that moves. 23,147,209 is #1793D1, the blue Arch uses for its own logo.
//
// The leading `0;` turns bold off before the colour is applied. fastfetch emits
// ESC[1m ahead of every logo line, and a terminal that draws bold brighter lifts
// the mark off #1793D1 toward cyan. Resetting first makes what is on screen the
// colour that was asked for.
//
// `modules` has to be here, and leaving it out is what broke this the first
// time. A config file is fastfetch's WHOLE configuration, not a patch on top of
// the defaults: with only a `logo` key it printed the logo and not one line of
// system information, and said nothing about why. The list below is fastfetch's
// own default order, read back from `--format json` rather than typed from
// memory. Adding a module means adding it here; the defaults no longer apply.
{
    "logo": {
        "color": {
            "1": "0;38;2;23;147;209"
        }
    },
    "modules": [
        "title",
        "separator",
        "os",
        "host",
        "kernel",
        "uptime",
        "packages",
        "shell",
        "display",
        "de",
        "wm",
        "wmtheme",
        "theme",
        "icons",
        "font",
        "cursor",
        "terminal",
        "terminalfont",
        "cpu",
        "gpu",
        "memory",
        "swap",
        "disk",
        "localip",
        "battery",
        "poweradapter",
        "locale",
        "break",
        "colors"
    ]
}
